encounters\", 0))\r\n40:             self.session_encounters = 0\r\n41:             self.session_dittos = 0\r\n42:             self.notifier = WebhookNotifier(self)\r\n43:             \r\n44:             self.mode_instance = self._initialize_mode()\r\n\n<truncated 14975 bytes>\n                st = time.time()\r\n346:                 while (time.time() - st) < 9.0:\r\n347:                     if not self.running: return\r\n348:                     time.sleep(1.0)\r\n349:                 \r\n350:                 # Si tras 9s el timer no se reseteó (nadie leyó nada exitoso), limpiamos\r\n351:                 if time.time() - self.last_activity_time > 50:\r\n352:                     self.log(\"GUARDIAN: Recovery sequence initiated.\", \"FATAL\")\r\n353:                     self.controller._press('x')\r\n354:                     time.sleep(0.5)\r\n355:                     self.controller.run_away()\r\n356:             \r\n357:             self.reset_activity_timer()\r\n358: \r\n359:     def loop(self):\r\n360:         self.start_time = datetime.now()\r\n361:         self.logger.clear_start_time()\r\n362:         self.reset_activity_timer()\r\n363:         \r\n364:         last_captcha_check = 0\r\n365:         \r\n366:         while self.running:\r\n367:             try:\r\n368:                 frame = self.observer.capture_frame()\r\n369:                 if frame is None: continue\r\n370:                 \r\n371:                 # Check de CAPTCHA de Emergencia cada 5s\r\n372:                 if time.time() - last_captcha_check > 5.0:\r\n373:                     if self.check_captcha(frame):\r\n374:                         break # Termina el bucle inmediatamente por seguridad\r\n375:                     last_captcha_check = time.time()\r\n376:                 \r\n377:                 self.check_guardian(frame)\r\n378:                 self.mode_instance.execute(frame)\r\n379:                 time.sleep(0.1)\r\n380:             except Exception as e:\r\n381:                 self.log(f\"LOOP ERROR: {e}\", \"FATAL\")\r\n382:                 time.sleep(1.0)\r\n383: \r\n384:     def start(self):\r\n385:         self.running = True; self.thread = Thread(target=self.loop, daemon=True); self.thread.start()\r\n386: \r\n387:     def stop(self):\r\n388:         self.running = False; self.save_progress()\r\n389: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":208,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-05T18:08:53Z","input_tokens":58816,"cache_read_tokens":16415,"output_tokens":5068,"thinking":"**Analyzing Menu Readiness**\n\nI've been examining the `is_menu_ready` function within `bot_main.py`. The focus is now on how this function determines whether a menu is ready for interaction, specifically by assessing a cropped region of the current frame based on configuration parameters. I'm looking at potential edge cases or areas for optimization within this decision logic.\n\n\n**Refining OCR Logic**\n\nI'm now diving deeper into the `is_menu_ready` function, specifically how it uses OCR to detect battle menus, after some feedback. It's becoming clearer that inaccurate OCR results could lead to the bot misinterpreting its battle state. I am focusing on the implications of false negatives and how the dual-check in `single.py` may be failing. I need to figure out why this logic is faltering.\n\n\n**Investigating Capture Failures**\n\nI'm now investigating the potential for false negatives in the capture sequence. If the Pokemon name OCR fails AND the menu check fails, the bot will patrol. I'm focusing on the `check_msg_area` function, and why it is not registering the success message when a Ditto is caught. It seems that OCR failure in this critical area leads to the bot incorrectly assuming the capture failed, continuing to patrol. I'm looking at how the \"CAUGHT!\" message detection is handled.\n\n\n**Unraveling Capture Timing**\n\nI've just realized a critical timing issue in the capture sequence, specifically with Ditto. The `_wait_for_menu` function, meant to detect the menu after a catch, waits for 30 seconds, even if the Pokemon is successfully captured. This delay explains the observed \"tarda muuucho en tocar x\" - it's a 30-second pause before the bot presses 'X' for cleanup. This needs immediate attention to streamline the capture process.\n\n\n**Pinpointing the UI Issue**\n\nI've got it! The bot patrols because it's missing the Ditto capture result UI, even after a successful catch, due to the wait times. It's skipping OCR of the chat altogether, relying on the visual \"Caught\" overlay or Pokedex UI. The issue is that the `\n<truncated 2006 bytes>\n*\n\nI'm now focusing on refining the `run_away()` function to address OCR failures and ensure reliable escape sequences. I'm thinking I can improve this by pressing `x` multiple times, then navigating to \"Run\". A key question is whether the OCR failures stem from a bad cropping region, preventing the \"Run\" button detection. I'm now investigating the potential impacts of a poorly-defined region in `config.json` on menu readiness.\n\n\n**Refining Battle Cycle**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on why the bot believes it's out of battle. The core problem is the double OCR failure: `check_any_name_visible` and `is_menu_ready` both returning false. This logic leads to the bot incorrectly entering patrol mode. I'm focusing on the interaction of these two checks and the reasons for their OCR failures. I need to get this function to behave correctly.\n\n\n**Addressing Escape Robustness**\n\nI'm now focusing on strengthening the bot's escape from battles, especially after OCR failures. I'm thinking I can make `run_away()` more robust by retrying key presses to ensure the escape sequence registers. If OCR fails, I need to verify that the menu has disappeared AND that the screen has changed before assuming an escape. I'm also considering shortening scan delays or OCR check delays to reduce patrol time.\n\n\n**Accelerating Menu Response**\n\nI'm now optimizing menu response after capture, especially for Ditto. I'll reduce capture delays by assuming success when no menu appears, and then trigger the `_capture_sequence` cleanup. We'll add more 'X' keypresses to this sequence as a failsafe, as well as an improved menu check, for faster resolution.\n\n\n**Accelerating Escape Efficiency**\n\nI'm now implementing improvements to ensure reliable escapes, focusing on the `run_away` function. I will add extra delays between key presses to avoid lag issues and to ensure that the bot is properly navigating the menu to select \"Run\". Should a menu remain visible, a second attempt to flee is now triggered. Scan cycles will also be sped up to quicken the map response.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"Append":"false","ArtifactMetadata":"{\"RequestFeedback\":true,\"Summary\":\"Plan para arreglar el delay de captura del Ditto y los escapes fallidos.\",\"UserFacing\":true}","CodeContent":"\"## Descripción del Objetivo\\nSolucionar dos problemas críticos detectados en los logs y en tu reporte, manteniendo el código simple y sin mareos (Regla #11):\\n1. **El \\\"delay\\\" al atrapar un Ditto**: El bot no detectaba la captura exitosa porque la ventana de la Pokédex/Stats bloqueaba el OCR, forzándolo a esperar un timeout por seguridad de 30+ segundos antes de tocar 'X'.\\n2. **El \\\"Falso Patrullaje\\\" (Escape fallido)**: Al huir de un Pokémon que no es Ditto, si el juego tiene lag y las teclas no se registran, el bot cree que escapó y empieza a caminar contra la pared estando aún en batalla.\\n3. **El \\\"1scan\\\" más corto**: Optimizaremos los tiempos de espera para que sea más ágil.\\n\\n## Propuesta de Cambios\\n\\n---\\n\\n### `src/modes/base.py`\\nVamos a hacer que el bot sea más inteligente al deducir que atrapó algo, eliminando la espera inútil.\\n\\n#### [MODIFY] `_check_capture_result`\\nSi pasan 12 segundos (60 polls) después de tirar la Pokébola y el menú de batalla **no regresó**, asumiremos que el Ditto fue capturado (porque la UI tapó la pantalla). Retornaremos `True` inmediatamente.\\n\\n#### [MODIFY] `_capture_sequence`\\nEn la sección de \\\"Limpieza final\\\" (cuando la batalla termina), agregaremos una ráfaga de teclas 'X' con pequeñas pausas (para \\\"mashear\\\" la X) y así destruir sin piedad la UI del Ditto o la Pokédex.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### `src/controller.py`\\nEl problema de que no escapa bien es mecánico (las teclas van demasiado rápido y el juego las ignora).\\n\\n#### [MODIFY] `run_away()`\\nAgregaremos pequeños `time.sleep(0.1)` entre los movimientos de las flechas (`up`, `left`, `right`, `down`) para darle tiempo al menú del juego a mover el cursor antes de presionar la tecla de confirmación (`z`).\\n\\n---\\n\\n### `src/modes/single.py`\\nEvitaremos el \\\"Falso Patrullaje\\\" validando la huida.\\n\\n#### [MODIFY] `_wait_for_map()`\\nSi después de mandar el comando de huir (`run_away`), el menú sigue visible... ¡significa que el escape falló por lag! En lugar de romper el ciclo y patrullar, l\n<truncated 729 bytes>","Description":"\"Plan para resolver delay de captura y problemas de huida.\"","Overwrite":"true","TargetFile":"\"C:\\\\Users\\\\Brian\\\\.gemini\\\\antigravity-cli\\\\brain\\\\dab6a3d4-72ff-45ea-898a-cdc9ce4a7ca1\\\\plan_fix_ditto_delay.md\"","toolAction":"\"Writing fix plan\"","toolSummary":"\"Plan creation\""}}],"truncated_fields":["thinking","tool_calls"]}
